<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zeitz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zeitz is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-elster">White Elster</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Otto I made it the seat of a bishopric in 968, which moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a> in the 11th century. From 1656 to 1718 it was the capital of the Duchy of Saxe-Zeitz, whose dukes built the Moritzburg palace, and in 1815 it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zeitz.jpg" alt="Zeitz" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mewes at German Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zeitz_Schloss1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Junior Albertine lines were endowed in 1656–1657 with the duchies of Saxe-Weissenfels, Saxe-Merseburg and Saxe-<b>Zeitz</b>, which reverted to the electorate when they died out in the eighteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Otto I founded a monastery at Magdeburg in 937 and, after years of negotiation with the Pope and the Archbishop of Mainz, raised it to an archbishopric in 968, with suffragans at Merseburg, Meissen, <b>Zeitz</b> (later Naumburg), Brandenburg and Havelberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 1656, Maurice received <b>Zeitz</b> with the secularised bishopric of Naumburg and lands in the Vogtland and around Neustadt an der Orla, founding the Duchy of Saxe-Zeitz.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-zeitz">Duchy of Saxe-Zeitz</a></li><li><blockquote>When Gero died in 965 Otto I divided his march into smaller units: the Northern March, the Margraviate of Meissen, the March of Lusatia (later Margraviate of Lower Lusatia), and the marches of Merseburg and <b>Zeitz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/marca-geronis">Marca Geronis</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishopric was founded at <b>Zeitz</b> in 968 under Magdeburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-naumburg">Prince-Bishopric of Naumburg</a></li></ul></section>
